Chapter 4: My Job.

In that moment, staring my sister's caseworker in the eye, putting myself and my family at risk of angering the demigods who had ruined my life, my instincts weighed the risks at a level both deeply unconscious and inescapably clear, and I made a commitment because my sister needed me. It was strangely liberating to be laser-focused like that; my eldest daughter says she feels the same way about life-and-death combat. Even in a very complicated situation that should be terrifying, having a single, all-important goal isn't either cold or hot, but almost… colorless in emotion.

That is when I started my job—the job I still have: to advocate for my people. It’s not my sister who needs saving now. (She's incredibly happy — “successful,” by my reckoning. Very happy with Uncle, who she now cheekily calls her "ex-husband" and is legally her wife. She is a devoted parent to the four children they had together, as well as the five more she fathered on Uncle's younger sisters. She is well on her way to founding her own clan, just as Mother always wanted.)

But that doesn't mean I can stop. As modern medicine and pre-modern breeding patterns collide, more and more catgirls leave our home timeline to try and find food and shelter in the Pan-Sentient-Community: all too often just humans putting a "All Species Welcome" sticker on the door of a cafeteria that doesn't serve any non-vegan dairy. And so, new 'problems', new misunderstandings, new completely — avoidable — tragedies — are happening every day.

Of course, back then, back at that moment: would I have made the same choice if I had known just how hard it would be? Yes, actually, I think so.

Maybe it's vanity on my part: to think that even back then I would give up on so much; that I would defy not just the humans who held all the power, but even my mother's expectation of what being a strong woman _means_. By her mother's standards, I am a failure: at best, a quixotic heroine; at worst, a confused child who refused to ever really grow up. I have only a single granddaughter, and her aunt, my eldest daughter, is married to a human man, together living out a macho fantasy of being a war leader not for a season, but for a lifetime.

I’ve had years when every effort felt useless, when nothing I did seemed to make anything better and it felt like the whole world hated me for daring to care about people, but -- I don't regret the choices I made, beginning at that moment. I don't regret doing the right thing.

If there's one thing I want you to take from this book, it's not "Catgirls life cycles are fundamentally different from primate sapients"; it's not even "Maybe in a situation where you have no real idea what is going on, you shouldn't default to the most extreme action possible"; It's that: "It's okay to do the right thing, even when it hurts".

***

The only one of the three things I demanded that my sister get that is actually _needed_ for our pregnancies is the butter. If you don't give us some proof that there is someone who our babies can nurse, then we _will_ miscarry. Its just not worth the risk. Butter is basically a way to trick our bodies into thinking there's more milk available than is actually being used to make the butter, a super stimulus if you will. Unaged cheeses, or just a glass of whole milk every day will also work. As for the cow-heart, that's just that we A) always need more meat then you do, and B) think its the best "cut" of beef.

Despite what some of our healers say, we don’t _need_ to drink semen. And despite what others claim, no, we can’t extract significant nutrition through our vaginas. But when your unborn baby is trying to drain your life force through its belly button—or when the smell of your mother’s tea, or even roofing tar three blocks away, is enough to make you vomit as if you’d given birth through your nose—being able to crawl into your husband’s arms, and have him show he still loves you and your body even as it punishes you… is very, nice. It helps; it helps a lot.

You might think that not being able to taste sweet would mean we wouldn't like fruit; and indeed many of the nonsentient members of our clade, who specialized in meat just a bit more, don't. It is true that our diets have a lot more nuts/oils/creamy plant products then the clasical starchy sweet fruit like an sweet apple or mild lemon, but we also actually can smell more flavors then you do, and are just as crazy about strange flavors. We love hot pepers like you do. Those of use who can eat chocolate and not throw up, or get the runs, love it (the lucky bastards). Tea, coffee, bayleaves, mint, pretty much all your spices, we love them. We can even grow to like the alergic reaction celery gives us. My mothers cooking was doused in mint for example. We are biological carnivours like wolves, we really cant go vegan for more then about 6 months and pushing that in our bearing years can actually in perminant sterility. (Mostly though we will just get constipaited and sleepy and kind of ... dumb.) but like racoons or bears, we crave varity and a wide range of food.

The situation that let to my mother emmegrating is saddly common among poeple who humanity is trying to help. Humans come in and give us vaccines and medicines and nutritional gummies, and so every single family in the whole area decides that clearly right now is a boom time, and the thing to do is have as many daughters as possible to help farm or work or trade. And then those daughters grow up and decide the same thing. And then those daughters and grand daughters grow up, and suddenly there's not land nor opprotunity for later daughters or traveling 'sons',

When my mother was growing up they delt with this in the traditional way. Give only enough food to the youngest girls to keep them alive, deliberately give them just meat, no dairy, not much fat, very little nuts and fruit. This keeps them alive, but also stops their ovulation, and causes any that are pregnant to miscarry.

And so, when my mother came to Canada, and found that her job let her afford basically as much exotic fruit and milk-fats as she could possibly want, she fell in love with a dozen different ones, and her, and mine, and my sister's, favorite is pineapple.